      "statusChangeNewStatus": "closed",
      "value": "I think Wub is confusing something here with motion vectors, the real answer here is that Anti Aliasing is simply an expensive technique. For VR purposes, most algorithms such as TSAA used in modern desktop games do not work (and can't work), and the working one (MSAA) that VRChat uses scales by screen resolution, so gets expensive at VR resolutions as you discovered. Anti aliasing is also a full-screen post processing technique, so it can't be separated for world/avatar/UI purposes.\n\nOne way to find the sweet spot for you is to disable anti aliasing and play with VR render resolutions in SteamVR etc. instead.\n\nIf someone discovers a VR-native anti aliasing solution with better performance, we will happily consider it :)",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
